 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500307 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500307 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500307 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500307 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the lines and curves \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500307 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c4c2566-13c3-50ff-974c-e99594494c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500307 #edb/ref 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,{e}^{x+y}=2,\\,{e}^{x+y}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,{e}^{x+y}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv={e}^{uy+y}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡v=uy+y\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500362 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500362 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500362 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=3x,\\,y=5x.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to ({x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d284dc90-b074-5607-a72d-8d0fe39284a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500362 #edb/ref 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=3x,\\,y=5x\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,{x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\frac{y}{x}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500364 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n${(x+3y)}^{2}+y=5$ and the line $y=4.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+3y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d78c4c29-1d34-52bf-9160-d17820674c44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500364 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+3y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+3y)}^{2}+y=5\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=5\\,⟹\\,v=5-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=5-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,4).$\n\n![](images/94/9427985b45271bec39577b35334311c11b71983d1cc5f8b9ac35c6fe88f29d98.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,4\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500369 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2y-5\\le x\\le 3+2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x-2y$ and $v=y-3$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3b9d256-a56c-59c5-89ce-db8a163e82f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500369 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$  \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2y-5\\le x\\le 3+2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,(2y-5)-2y\\le x-2y\\le (3+2y)-2y,\\,0-3\\le y-3\\le 4-3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 3,\\,-3\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500373 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(2x-3y)^{2}+y=3$ and the line $y=0.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=2x-3y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eabf1331-ca5b-594d-af21-53db9e3ebf21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500373 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $2x-3y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-3y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0).$\n\n![](images/17/172daaae3de5212c5e182965b66bf2afcade59e2d5eb156c7dbff214e3bfb040.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500388 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c70884a-daa4-52bf-a53a-39f975c92f7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500388 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500390 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500390 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "148c81a9-4b53-5838-b141-ec4a2153b3c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500390 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500407 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500407 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c991136-4174-5b8c-a072-9dbe197bf649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500407 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500411 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500411 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6df31d5a-d97b-5683-b5dd-1ec918e08ea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500411 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500422 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186500411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500422 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c534e8b-12d5-5861-8fad-aaddc6760864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500422 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186500373 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
